What to Eat During Your Period: Best Foods for Menstrual Health

Your period is a natural part of the menstrual cycle, but it can sometimes come with tiredness, cramps, bloating, mood changes, and food cravings. While there is no single food that can completely prevent period symptoms, eating a balanced and nutritious diet can support your overall health and help you feel more comfortable during menstruation.

Include Iron-Rich Foods

Iron is an important nutrient during menstruation because the body loses some blood during a period. People with heavy menstrual bleeding may have a higher risk of low iron levels.

Good sources of iron include spinach, lentils, chickpeas, beans, tofu, eggs, meat, and iron-fortified foods. If you mainly eat plant-based foods, combining iron-rich foods with vitamin C sources such as oranges, tomatoes, or lemons may help improve iron absorption.

Add Protein to Your Meals

Protein helps support the body's normal functions and can be included in every main meal. Eggs, fish, chicken, beans, lentils, paneer, tofu, yoghurt, nuts, and seeds are useful options.

For breakfast, you could choose eggs with whole-grain toast or oats with nuts and seeds. For lunch and dinner, meals containing dal, vegetables, rice or chapati, and a protein source can provide a balanced combination of nutrients.

Eat Plenty of Fruits and Vegetables

Fruits and vegetables provide vitamins, minerals, fibre, and water. Bananas, oranges, apples, berries, watermelon, spinach, carrots, cucumber, tomatoes, and other seasonal produce can be included in your daily meals.

Rather than focusing on one “best period food,” try to eat a variety of foods throughout the month.

Choose Whole Grains

Whole grains such as oats, brown rice, whole-wheat bread, and millets provide carbohydrates and fibre. They can help make meals more filling and provide a steady source of energy.

You do not need to completely avoid rice, chapati, or other carbohydrates during your period. The goal is simply to maintain a balanced diet.

Stay Hydrated

Drinking enough water is important throughout your menstrual cycle. Some people experience bloating during their period and may mistakenly think that drinking less water will help. Staying adequately hydrated is generally a better approach.

Water, soups, and water-rich fruits can all contribute to your daily fluid intake.

What About Period Cravings?

Craving chocolate, sweets, salty snacks, or comfort foods during your period is common. You do not necessarily have to eliminate these foods. Instead, try to enjoy them in moderation while continuing to eat balanced meals.

For example, dark chocolate, fruit with yoghurt, nuts, or homemade snacks can be alternatives when you want something sweet.

Good Menstrual Hygiene Matters Too

Food is only one part of menstrual wellness. Choosing a comfortable and suitable sanitary pad can also make period days easier.

Different people have different flow levels, so you may need different pad sizes during different days of your cycle. Longer and higher-absorbency pads can be useful during heavier-flow days, while lighter options may be suitable for lighter days.

